    "comments": [
      {
        "id": "comment-1",
        "text": "FOR LOOP\n\nFor Loop runs its Body pin Count times, then continues. Add the numbers 1..10:\nthe loop body bumps a counter and adds it to a running total (total = 55 at the end).\nFor Loop has no index output; keep your own counter variable as shown here.",
        "position": [
          60.0,
          -300.0
        ],
        "size": [
          900.0,
          210.0
        ],
        "color": [
          0.2,
          0.4,
          0.8,
          0.3
        ],
        "contained_node_ids": []
      },
      {
        "id": "comment-2",
        "text": "BODY, STEP 1\ni = i + 1. The first pass makes i = 1, the last i = 10.",
        "position": [
          360.0,
          460.0
        ],
        "size": [
          560.0,
          90.0
        ],
        "color": [
          0.2,
          0.65,
          0.35,
          0.3
        ],
        "contained_node_ids": [
          "gi",
          "i1",
          "si"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "comment-3",
        "text": "BODY, STEP 2\ntotal = total + i. After ten passes total = 1+2+...+10 = 55.",
        "position": [
          660.0,
          630.0
        ],
        "size": [
          620.0,
          90.0
        ],
        "color": [
          0.9,
          0.55,
          0.15,
          0.3
        ],
        "contained_node_ids": [
          "gt",
          "gi2",
          "sum",
          "st"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "comment-4",
        "text": "THE LOOP\nCount = 10 is typed on the node. Wire any integer here to loop a computed number of times. For Loop has just a Body output (no Completed pin), so work that must happen after the loop belongs in another event or in a macro.",
